Validation update 2026-10-17 final: T3 main now contains the reviewed live verifier commits 64f7fb99, 5d34d52b, and 66f803a0. Using Node 24.13.1, Codex 0.163.0 SHA256 399ff69c431c066e40de318fba45c2b07f45a76b19939d247ee4828fc70c4215, and c2c 0.18.2 host-enabled binary SHA256 246d2dfdf793b4b3e26d4b5ca0e5a48c1be883f53b080f37583a74f68496909a, the isolated verifier passed both scenarios: idle local DATA created exactly one continuation run and active local DATA stayed in the original run. Each receipt reached delivered, target binding was derived read-only, recipient used exactly one namespaced t3-code.c2c_configure call, and no polling tools/commands were observed. Evidence: /tmp/t3-c2c-live-cCghX7 (idle) and /tmp/t3-c2c-live-8IIx6e (active). The remote full c2c check was attempted from the exact tree but exited 124 on its bounded Dune watchdog/queue timeout after prolonged FUSE compilation; focused c2c/T3 suites and the live receipts are green. No production push or PR was made.

Scope note: the committed verifier and receipts cover the initial two-case vertical slice specified in the harness plan (idle local wake and active same-run DATA). The broader follow-on matrix named in the original research (same-dir multi-thread isolation, restart, DND/stop, native-child, malicious/ephemeral/deferrable, unsupported adapters) remains a separate follow-up validation tranche; this closeout does not claim those live cases were exercised.